<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- ACT I: THE LAST MORNING & THE BEGINNING                 -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="transition">FADE IN:</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - COURTYARD - MORNING (MAY 27, 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Cold spring light on the baroque facade. An open dark-green MERCEDES 320-B idles on the gravel.</p>
  <p class="action">REINHARD HEYDRICH (38) stands by the door — tall, blade-thin, immaculate General’s tunic, unclasped briefcase under one arm. His voice, when he speaks, is higher than most expect from the face.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA HEYDRICH (30), heavily pregnant, wraps a wool shawl tighter. One final embrace. She holds on a second longer than he does.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Two days in Berlin. No longer.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Two days. Then straight back to you and the children.</div>

  <p class="action">He climbs into the open passenger seat beside SS-Oberscharführer KLEIN. Turns. Raises a gloved hand. Lina waves until the gravel spray settles.</p>
  <p class="action">Beside the portal, MARTA the maid dabs her eyes.</p>

  <div class="character">MARTA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(voice shaking)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Oh, gnädige Frau… what an ominous departure.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(without turning)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Marta. Superstition is for people who have nothing else to do. Go inside.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - DRAWING ROOM - AFTERNOON (3:00 PM)</div>
  <p class="action">Lina sits sewing baby garments. Boots click. DR. HORST BÖHME (Prague SiPo Commander) stands in the doorway, helmet in hand, ash-pale.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Dr. Böhme? Is something wrong with your wife?</div>

  <div class="character">BÖHME</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(choked)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">On your husband… an assassination attempt has been carried out.</div>

  <p class="action">The needle freezes in her fingers. Silence.</p>

  <div class="character">BÖHME (CONT'D)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He lives. Bulovka Hospital in Libeň. Professors are operating. A car is waiting.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">An assassination. The word went through me. Yet I felt completely numb. Our life had taught us to expect such surprises. Being Chief of the Gestapo was never an enviable trade in Adolf Hitler’s Reich. I thought of the children — Klaus, Heider, Silke — and the one still unborn. Now it would fall on me.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE: HOSPITAL & FLASHBACK TRIGGER                      -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. BULOVKA HOSPITAL - CORRIDOR / WARD - LATE AFTERNOON</div>
  <p class="action">Professor Gerhardt, the German ambassador, meets her. Professor Knaus, her own doctor, is already there. She is led into the room.</p>
  <p class="action">Reinhard has been operated on twice. Conscious. Barely able to speak.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(whisper)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Denk an unsere Kinder… Think of our children.</div>

  <p class="action">She is led out on unsteady legs. Knaus gives her an injection.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I was allowed to bring him bouillon I cooked myself. Every day he seemed better. Until the night of the third to the fourth of June.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - BEDROOM - NIGHT (JUNE 3–4)</div>
  <p class="action">She is torn from sleep. Cold. Teeth chattering. Hands shaking. Her bag falls; she leaves it. Nothing matters.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BULOVKA HOSPITAL - WARD - DAWN</div>
  <p class="action">He is already in a coma. They pull her away. Sedative. When she wakes — he is gone.</p>

  <div class="character">RADIO ANNOUNCER (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The Obergruppenführer and General of Police, Chief of the Security Police and SD, Acting Reichsprotektor of Bohemia and Moravia, Reinhard Heydrich, has today succumbed to his injuries.</div>

  <p class="action">Lina sits alone in the hospital corridor, hands folded over the unborn child.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Fifteen years earlier I met the man who became my fate. Everything that followed — the power, the fear, the ridicule, the silence — began with a ball and a quiet proposal in a wine cellar.</div>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- ACT II: KIEL 1930 — THE MEETING                          -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. KIEL - HENRIETTENHAUS - ROOM 18 - DAY (DECEMBER 1930)</div>
  <p class="action">Four young women, bored, broke, cake and malt coffee gone. LINA (19), sharp-eyed, restless.</p>

  <div class="character">GIRL</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Lina, tell us about your aunts again.</div>

  <p class="action">She does the usual caricatures, but her heart is not in it. A ball is coming. A boy from the neighboring village has cancelled. She almost stays home. The others make her keep her word.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. TONHALLE KIEL - BALLROOM - NIGHT (DECEMBER 6, 1930)</div>
  <p class="action">Mostly girls. Few dancers. Lina and two friends sit in the background, ready to leave. Then two naval officers cut through the crowd. One dark and aristocratic (VON MANSTEIN). The other extraordinarily tall, blond, icy eyes, parted hair.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(clicking heels, formal bow — voice clear, slightly high)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Oberleutnant zur See Reinhard Heydrich. May I have this dance?</div>

  <p class="action">They dance. Later he walks her to the door of the Henriettenhaus and asks to see her again.</p>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. HOHENZOLLERNPARK - DAY (TWO DAYS LATER)</div>
  <p class="action">They walk the length and breadth of the park. He talks of his mother, his childhood in Halle, the conservatory, the lost family fortune. No flirtation. No performance. Only a quiet, intense desire to be known.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">There was nothing of the lover trying to please. He gave himself as comrade, as friend — and yet it was already more. When I came home I was no longer the same person.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. SMALL WINE CELLAR - BRUNSWICK STREET - NIGHT</div>
  <p class="action">After the theatre (she cannot remember the play). They sit in near silence. She feels ordinary beside him. Then, calmly:</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Will you be my wife?</div>

  <p class="action">Silence. Her mind races: schoolgirl, poor village teacher’s daughter, his navy regulations, her parents’ expectations.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">My God, Herr Heydrich — you don’t even know my parents. You know nothing of my father’s profession. You are a naval officer. There are rules.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(smiling, quiet)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I do not want to marry your father. I want to marry you.</div>

  <p class="action">She tells him everything. Then she says yes.</p>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE: ENGAGEMENT & THE FIRST CRACK                       -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. LÜTJENBRODE SCHOOLHOUSE - CHRISTMAS 1930</div>
  <p class="action">Red brick village school. Her father, the self-assured Dorfschulmeister, and Reinhard size each other up. Her father talks of Fehmarn seafarers, gold-rush grandfathers, inflation, the Church as worldly power. Reinhard listens with the same intensity he later brings to maps and card indexes.</p>

  <div class="character">FATHER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">(to Lina later)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He fits our landscape.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I did not yet know that the navy had its own landscape — and that it was about to reject him.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. KIEL - NAVAL OFFICERS’ QUARTERS / LATER, A ROOM IN THE HENRIETTENHAUS</div>
  <p class="action">Reinhard pale, quiet. He has been summoned before a court of honor. A girl from a colonial women’s school. A sailing regatta. A promise of marriage he did not keep. Comrades who pressed the case. The chairman of the court is one of his own.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">In the navy they have a pigtail for everything. Other cases were worse. One comrade’s official fiancée stayed with him when he was ill. He was finished too.</div>

  <p class="action">Lina presses him. She never fully believes the version that nothing physical happened. The outcome is clear: forced resignation. Two years’ transitional pay. Career ended.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">If he had not left the navy in 1931, only God knows what would have become of us. The same men who later called him cold and ambitious were the ones who first closed the door.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. HALLE - HEYDRICH FAMILY APARTMENT - 1931</div>
  <p class="action">His mother’s face collapses when she hears. She had pinned her last hopes on this son. The house still shows traces of former wealth — porcelain, silver — but the radio has ruined the music business and the inflation has finished the rest. Bruno Heydrich, the former Heldentenor, is a large, theatrical man who still lives in the center of every room. Elisabeth is small, precise, cultured, and holds the household together with a pencil and a daily shopping list.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They said later he had a high voice, that he was vain, that comrades betrayed him because he was unbearable. I saw a man whose family had already fallen once, and who was falling again.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- ACT III: MUNICH — THE SKETCH THAT BECAME AN EMPIRE       -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">EXT. WALDTRUDERING NEAR MUNICH - DUSK (JUNE 1931)</div>
  <p class="action">Muddy lane. Ramshackle house. Chickens. Baron von Eberstein escorts Reinhard — now in a threadbare civilian suit — up the steps.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. HIMMLER BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS</div>
  <p class="action">HEINRICH HIMMLER (30) sits propped up in bed, wire spectacles, feverish. Nightshirt.</p>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The Baron speaks highly of your naval service. But we don’t need sailors. We need counterintelligence. The Party is full of police spies and communists.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">In the Reichsmarine I specialized in radio and secure communications.</div>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(sliding pen and blank notepad across the blanket)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Thirty minutes. Sketch me an independent intelligence service for the SS.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. PARLOR - 25 MINUTES LATER</div>
  <p class="action">Reinhard’s hand moves with mechanical precision: hierarchical branches, card indexes, courier lines, counter-surveillance cells.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BEDROOM - MOMENTS LATER</div>
  <p class="action">Himmler studies the sheet. Eyebrows rise behind the round lenses.</p>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">One hundred and eighty marks a month. You report to me. Can you move to Munich at once?</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Yes, Reichsführer.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I saw it as a young unemployed officer taking technical work. He saw a structure that could be built. Later people said the sketch itself was an act of pure ambition. He never spoke of it that way to me. He spoke of order.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. MUNICH - NYMPHENBURG HOUSE - NIGHT (1932)</div>
  <p class="action">Illegal period. SA and SS banned. The house is arranged for sudden visitors: compromising papers can vanish in seconds. The dog is the early-warning system. Lina’s bed is a kind of fortress. She heats the coke stoves every morning before dawn. No help can be kept — too dangerous, and no money.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I was cook, cleaner, lookout, and alarm. When Röhm and Himmler came to inspect, I had swapped the ordinary matches for exploding ones as a joke on the men who kept stealing them. The bang nearly caused a diplomatic incident. Röhm laughed until he cried. A year later he stood godfather to our first son.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE: DOMESTIC LIFE & THE MAN BEHIND THE IMAGE           -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. BERLIN - SCHLACHTENSEE HOUSE - MUSIC ROOM - EVENING (1938)</div>
  <p class="action">Fire burning. Reinhard and ERIKA CANARIS play a Bach double concerto with razor precision. ADMIRAL WILHELM CANARIS sips Mosel beside Lina.</p>

  <div class="character">CANARIS</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(wry)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">If only the Abwehr and the SD were as finely tuned as those two violins.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(lowering the bow, unsmiling)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Harmony requires discipline. When one instrument plays out of turn, it must be retuned. Or replaced.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">People later said he had no friends. That is true. He believed his work forbade them. After the navy court and after Röhm, he asked Himmler never to offer the familiar “Du.” He was afraid that friendship would blunt the force of his objections. “You are my best friend,” he told me once. “We do not need to share with anyone.”</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. SAME HOUSE - MORNING ROUTINE</div>
  <p class="action">Reinhard rises early. A barber comes every morning; Lina dislikes him. Newspaper during the shave. Breakfast together. Precisely at eight he is at the office. First stop: the fencing hall. Daily bouts. He had been German team champion; after that it was only maintenance of the body.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They mocked the high voice. They said he was an upstart who had been thrown out of the navy for chasing skirts and then invented a secret police to avenge himself. I lived with the man who practiced Bach in the evenings, who flew aerobatics over Fehmarn to land on a meadow and eat fried potatoes and Krum und Milch, who could not bear to hear the Toselli Serenade because the petty officers had forced him to play it drunk when he was a young officer. Everything he loved and everything he hated he put into that violin.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- ACT IV: PRAGUE — THE LAST MONTHS                         -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - STUDY - NIGHT (JANUARY 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Large map of Russia unrolled. Reinhard traces the Urals and Siberia with a red pencil. Lina enters with tea.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">You are barely home and already buried in papers. What is this conference in Wannsee you have summoned everyone to?</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(clinical, detached)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The final solution of the European Jewish question. We are organizing the complete transfer to the East.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">To Siberia? Millions of people? How could they survive in those barrens?</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Siberia has coal, ore, timber. The strong will build the roads. Naturally a great percentage will fall away through natural attrition. The rest will work. It is an immense engineering problem, Lina. Nothing more.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">That is what he told me. I believed him. Later a friend who had seen the shooting squads wrote that the resettlement had become something else. By then Reinhard was already dead.</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. PRAGUE-HOLEŠOVICE - TRAM LOOP CURVE - 10:35 AM (MAY 27, 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Hairpin bend. Streetcar groans. JAN KUBIŠ leans on the railing, hand inside raincoat on an anti-tank grenade. Opposite: JOSEF GABČÍK, trench coat over a folded STEN.</p>
  <p class="action">A pocket mirror flashes twice. Signal.</p>
  <p class="action">The open green Mercedes descends. Klein slows for the tight turn.</p>
  <p class="action">Gabčík steps into the street. Raises the Sten. Aims at Heydrich. Pulls the trigger.</p>
  <p class="action">CLICK. Misfire. Bolt jammed.</p>

  <div class="character">KLEIN</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(shouting)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Herr Obergruppenführer!</div>

  <p class="action">Instead of ordering Klein to accelerate, Heydrich RISES in the open car. Face white with fury. He draws his 7.65 mm pistol and aims point-blank at Gabčík.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Shoot the dog!</div>

  <p class="action">Behind the car Kubiš steps forward and hurls the grenade underhand.</p>
  <p class="action">KABOOM. Rear wheel lifts. Smoke, metal, horsehair, dust.</p>
  <p class="action">Heydrich staggers out through the passenger door, pistol firing blindly into the fumes, then collapses face-down against the warm radiator grille.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They said later it was ego that made him stand up instead of fleeing. I know only that the man I married did not know how to duck.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE: DEATHBED & FUNERAL                                 -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. BULOVKA HOSPITAL - WARD - NIGHT (JUNE 3, 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Lina beside the high iron bed. Reinhard’s face sunken, feverish. The grenade splinter has turned septic.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(barely audible)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Lina… go back to Fehmarn. Take the children home.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Hush. You will come home with us.</div>

  <p class="action">His eyes roll upward. Dry lips move, quoting his father’s opera:</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(faint)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Yes, the world is a barrel organ… that the good Lord turns himself… and everyone must dance to the tune on the drum…</div>

  <p class="action">Monitors flatline. A doctor pulls her back by the shoulders.</p>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. INVALIDENFRIEDHOF BERLIN / MOSAIC HALL REICH CHANCELLERY - DAY</div>
  <p class="action">State funeral. Wagner’s Götterdämmerung. Hitler and Himmler. The two older boys stand in for their mother. A simple soldier’s grave with a wooden cross and his steel helmet.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He had always felt himself a soldier. That is how he died. Ten days later I turned thirty-two. A young widow, “rich in children,” as they used to say. My father had said at my birth: “She came into the world without help; she will go through the world without help.” I remembered that in the autumn of 1945 when I stood half-naked and penniless at the door of my parents’ house on Fehmarn, the wife of a war criminal.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- ACT V: AFTERMATH — THE WOMAN WHO REMAINED                -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - JULY 1942</div>
  <p class="action">Daughter MARTE is born in the night of 22–23 July. Lina holds a fatherless child. Himmler appoints himself guardian. Hitler offers the castle. Complicated papers. She refuses to accept conditions that would forbid her remarriage. She stays as a kind of land-office employee so the children can remain.</p>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. GERMANY IN COLLAPSE - SPRING–AUTUMN 1945</div>
  <p class="action">Flight from Prague. Czech staff help pack the car. One of them, Resi, says at parting: “Don’t cry, Excellency. We will see each other again.” Years later Lina learns she was killed by her own people as a collaborator.</p>
  <p class="action">Forged papers. Black market. Children smuggled north in a hollow artificial leg filled with money and cigars. Checkpoints. “Please, I have no time.” Salad and cucumber in a net to look like a housewife on an errand. Eighteen checkpoints passed.</p>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. FEHMARN - PARENTS’ HOUSE - AUTUMN 1945</div>
  <p class="action">She knocks. Half-naked, three children, nothing left. The wife of a Kriegsverbrecher.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Old friends crossed the street. Authorities told me I had no claim to a widow’s pension because I had been a beneficiary of the regime. All that could be proved was that I was Reinhard Heydrich’s widow. Nothing more.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BERLIN TRIBUNAL ROOM - DAY (CIRCA 1960)</div>
  <p class="action">Cold institutional chamber. Flashbulbs. Eight hundred schoolboys in the gallery for political education. LINA (now 49), austere in black, sits with her lawyer and her youngest daughter MARTE (18).</p>

  <div class="character">JUDGE</div>
  <div class="dialogue">…the deceased Reinhard Heydrich was not merely an official obeying directives but an initiator of systematic mass murder. The estate remains confiscated. The petitioner has no claim to a general military widow’s pension.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(whispering to Marte, voice steel)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Look at them. In 1941 the whole country threw flowers under his wheels. Now they bring schoolboys to watch the widow of the monster pay for their sins.</div>

  <div class="character">MARTE</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Was he what they say he was, Mother?</div>

  <p class="action">Lina’s hand touches the pearls at her neck. A long pause.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He was a soldier who did his duty to the end. The rest was the storm.</div>

  <div class="transition">FADE OUT.</div>

  <div class="end-card">
    LINA HEYDRICH RETURNED TO THE BALTIC ISLAND OF FEHMARN.<br>
    SHE OPERATED A SMALL INN UNTIL HER DEATH IN 1985.<br>
    SHE MAINTAINED HER HUSBAND’S INNOCENCE TO THE END.<br>
    WITHOUT HER THERE WOULD BE NO BOOK.
  </div>

  <div class="time-estimate">
    ESTIMATED RUNTIME: 48–55 MINUTES
  </div>

