<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- OPENING: THE CONCERT AS DREAM THRESHOLD -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="transition">FADE IN:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. WALDSTEIN PALACE, PRAGUE - CONCERT HALL - NIGHT (26 MAY 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Soft amber light. A small orchestra plays music by RICHARD BRUNO HEYDRICH — the father. Reinhard sits beside Lina in the front row, upright, listening with that peculiar intensity he reserved for music and maps.</p>
  <p class="action">Lina watches his profile more than the stage. His hand rests on his knee. The high, clear voice is silent tonight.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I still see the light on the marble. I still hear the music his father wrote. And I still cannot decide whether any of it was real.</div>

  <p class="action">The music swells. Reinhard turns his head a fraction toward her. Almost a smile. Then the image softens, loses focus, as if the film itself is dissolving.</p>

  <div class="dream-note">— the dream begins —</div>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - COURTYARD - MORNING (27 MAY 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">The same face, now in uniform. The open green Mercedes waits. He is leaving for Berlin. Two days, he says. No longer.</p>
  <p class="action">She holds him a second longer than he holds her. Marta the maid is already weeping at the portal.</p>

  <div class="character">MARTA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Oh, gnädige Frau… what an ominous departure.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(quietly, almost to herself)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Superstition is for people who have nothing else to do.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Even then I felt it — that this morning did not quite belong to the world I had known.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- THE ATTACK INSIDE THE DREAM -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">EXT. PRAGUE-HOLEŠOVICE - TRAM LOOP CURVE - 10:35 AM</div>
  <p class="action">The hairpin bend. The streetcar. Two men waiting. The open Mercedes slows.</p>
  <p class="action">GABČÍK steps into the road, raises the Sten, pulls the trigger.</p>
  <p class="action">CLICK. Jammed.</p>
  <p class="action">Instead of ordering the driver to accelerate, Reinhard rises in the open car. Face white. Pistol drawn. He aims point-blank.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Shoot the dog!</div>

  <p class="action">Behind the car KUBIŠ throws the grenade underhand.</p>
  <p class="action">KABOOM. Smoke. Horsehair. Metal. Reinhard staggers out, still firing into the fumes, then collapses against the radiator.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They said later it was pride that made him stand up. I only know that the man I married did not know how to duck. And that in the dream he is still standing there, white-faced, firing at something he can no longer see.</div>

  <div class="transition">DISSOLVE TO:</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BULOVKA HOSPITAL - WARD - NIGHT (3–4 JUNE 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">She is allowed to sit beside him. He is already far away. The last clear words:</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(barely audible)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Denk an unsere Kinder…</div>

  <p class="action">Later, almost inaudible, a line from his father’s opera:</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Yes, the world is a barrel organ that the good Lord turns himself… and everyone must dance to the tune on the drum…</div>

  <p class="action">Then nothing. They pull her away.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I was not at the funeral in Berlin. The boys stood in my place. I stayed with the unborn child and the three who were already here. In the dream I sometimes walk into the Mosaic Hall and find the coffin empty. Only the steel helmet remains.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- THE LIFE REPLAYED AS MEMORY / DREAM -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. TONHALLE KIEL - BALLROOM - NIGHT (DECEMBER 1930)</div>
  <p class="action">A tall naval officer bows. The voice is clearer, higher than one expects from the face.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Oberleutnant zur See Reinhard Heydrich. May I have this dance?</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">That is where it began. Or where the dream began. I can no longer tell the difference.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. SMALL WINE CELLAR - BRUNSWICK STREET - NIGHT</div>
  <p class="action">After the theatre. Silence. Then, calmly:</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Will you be my wife?</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">My God, Herr Heydrich — you don’t even know my parents.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I do not want to marry your father. I want to marry you.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I said yes. And the navy court of honor ended his career a few months later. Comrades who pressed the case. A girl from a colonial school. A sailing regatta. He came home pale and quiet. The first time the world closed a door on him.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. HIMMLER BEDROOM - WALDTRUDERING - 1931</div>
  <p class="action">Himmler in a nightshirt, feverish. A blank notepad pushed across the blanket.</p>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Thirty minutes. Sketch me an independent intelligence service for the SS.</div>

  <p class="action">Twenty-five minutes later the page is covered with clean hierarchical lines.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They gave him one hundred and eighty marks a month. He thought he was building order. I thought he had found work. In the dream the sketch keeps growing until it covers the whole map of Europe.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- DOMESTIC FRAGMENTS -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. BERLIN - SCHLACHTENSEE HOUSE - MUSIC ROOM - EVENING</div>
  <p class="action">Firelight. Reinhard and ERIKA CANARIS play the Double Violin Concerto in D minor. Bach. The slow movement. Precise, almost severe, yet full of feeling.</p>
  <p class="action">Admiral Canaris sits beside Lina, listening.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">This is the sound I still hear when the house is empty. Not Wagner. Not the marches. Bach. The two violins answering each other across the room. He could be tender with the instrument in a way he was rarely tender with people outside the family.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. SAME HOUSE - MORNING</div>
  <p class="action">The barber. The newspaper. Breakfast. At eight he is gone. First the fencing hall. Then the office.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">They mocked the high voice. They said he was vain, that comrades had betrayed him because he was unbearable. I lived with the man who practiced Bach in the evenings and flew aerobatics over Fehmarn so he could land on a meadow and eat fried potatoes. Everything he loved and everything he hated went into that violin.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - STUDY - NIGHT (JANUARY 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">A map of Russia unrolled. Red pencil marks over the Urals and Siberia.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">What is this conference in Wannsee?</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The final solution of the European Jewish question. Transfer to the East. Siberia has coal, ore, timber. The strong will build the roads. Naturally a great percentage will fall away through natural attrition. It is an immense engineering problem, Lina. Nothing more.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">That is what he told me. I believed him. In the dream the map keeps expanding until there is no land left outside the red lines.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- AFTERMATH INSIDE THE DREAM -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - JULY 1942</div>
  <p class="action">Daughter Marte is born in the night of 22–23 July. A fatherless child. Himmler appoints himself guardian. Hitler offers the castle. She refuses conditions that would forbid her remarriage.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">I stayed so the children could stay. Later they said I was a beneficiary of the regime. All that could be proved was that I was his widow.</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. GERMANY IN COLLAPSE - 1945</div>
  <p class="action">Flight. Forged papers. Children hidden. Eighteen checkpoints. Salad and cucumber in a net so she looks like an ordinary housewife on an errand.</p>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. FEHMARN - PARENTS’ HOUSE - AUTUMN 1945</div>
  <p class="action">She knocks. Half-naked. Three children. Nothing left.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Old friends crossed the street. The authorities told me I had no claim to a widow’s pension. In the dream I sometimes stand at that door for years and no one opens.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BERLIN TRIBUNAL ROOM - DAY (CIRCA 1960)</div>
  <p class="action">Flashbulbs. Schoolboys in the gallery. Lina, now older, sits with her youngest daughter.</p>

  <div class="character">JUDGE</div>
  <div class="dialogue">…the deceased was not merely an official obeying directives but an initiator of systematic mass murder. The estate remains confiscated.</div>

  <div class="character">MARTE</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Was he what they say he was, Mother?</div>

  <p class="action">A long silence. Lina’s hand touches the pearls at her neck.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He was a soldier who did his duty to the end. The rest was the storm.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Even now, when I wake, I am never certain which part was the dream — the concert, the grenade, the violin, or the silence that followed.</div>

  <div class="transition">FADE OUT.</div>

  <div class="end-card">
    LINA HEYDRICH RETURNED TO FEHMARN.<br>
    SHE OPERATED A SMALL INN UNTIL HER DEATH IN 1985.<br>
    SHE MAINTAINED THAT IT HAD ALL BEEN A KIND OF DREAM<br>
    FROM WHICH SHE NEVER FULLY WOKE.<br>
    WITHOUT HER THERE WOULD BE NO BOOK.
  </div>

