<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 1: PRAGUE 1942 — THE DEPARTURE                     -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="transition">FADE IN:</div>

  <div class="slugline">EXT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - COURTYARD - MORNING (MAY 27, 1942)</div>
  <p class="action">Cold, crystalline spring sunlight hits the baroque facade. An open dark-green MERCEDES 320-B idle-purrs on the gravel.</p>
  <p class="action">REINHARD HEYDRICH (38) stands by the door. Tall, blade-thin, immaculate double-breasted General's tunic. Under his arm: an unclasped leather briefcase.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA HEYDRICH (30), heavily pregnant, wraps a woolen shawl tighter around herself. She pulls him close. One final embrace.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Two days in Berlin? No longer?</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Two days with the Führer. Then directly back to you and the children.</div>

  <p class="action">He climbs into the open front passenger seat beside SS-Oberscharführer KLEIN. Heydrich turns, raising a gloved hand. Lina waves back until the gravel spray settles.</p>
  <p class="action">Beside the portal, MARTA the maid dabs her eyes with a wet handkerchief.</p>

  <div class="character">MARTA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(voice shaking)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Oh, gnädige Frau... what an ominous departure.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(without turning around)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Marta. Don't be superstitious. Go inside.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. CASTLE JUNGFERN-BRESCHAN - DRAWING ROOM - AFTERNOON (3:00 PM)</div>
  <p class="action">Lina sits sewing baby garments. Through the heavy oak doors, boots click rapidly. DR. HORST BÖHME (Prague SiPo Commander) stands in the doorway. Ash-pale. Helmet in hand.</p>

  <div class="character">LINA</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Dr. Böhme? Is something wrong with your wife?</div>

  <div class="character">BÖHME</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(choked, unable to look her in the eye)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">On your husband... an assassination attempt has been carried out.</div>

  <p class="action">The needle pauses in Lina's fingers. A total, dead silence falls.</p>

  <div class="character">BÖHME (CONT'D)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">He lives. He is at the Bulovka Hospital in Libeň. Professors are operating now. A car is waiting for you.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">An assassination. The word pierced straight through me. Yet I felt completely numb. Our life had taught us to expect such surprises. Being Chief of the Gestapo was never an enviable trade.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 2: KIEL 1930 — THE NAVY & THE VIOLIN               -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">INT. KIEL ROWING CLUB - BALLROOM - NIGHT (DECEMBER 6, 1930)</div>
  <p class="action">FLASHBACK. Twelve years earlier. Brass band playing a lively waltz. Sea-cadets in formal dark-blue uniforms circle the dance floor.</p>
  <p class="action">LINA (19), a schoolteacher's daughter from Fehmarn, sits with two classmates. Bored. Watching the room.</p>
  <p class="action">TWO NAVAL OFFICERS cut through the crowd toward their table. One is dark-haired, aristocratic (VON MANSTEIN). The other is striking: extraordinarily tall, icy blue eyes, parted blond hair.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(clicking heels sharply, formal bow)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Oberleutnant zur See Reinhard Heydrich. May I have this dance?</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. NAVAL BARRACKS - KIEL-HOLTENAU - LATE NIGHT (1928)</div>
  <p class="action">Cramped naval officer's quarters. Rain lashes the glass. Reinhard sits on his cot in his undershirt, practicing a Bach partitas on a polished wooden VIOLIN.</p>
  <p class="action">SLAM! The door kicks open. PETTY OFFICER REHM stumbles in, reeking of schnapps, uniform half-unbuttoned.</p>

  <div class="character">REHM</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(slurring, aggressive)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Still fiddling, artist's brat? Up! Play the Toselli Serenade!</div>

  <p class="action">Young Reinhard freezes. His jaw tightens. His knuckles go stark white on the bow.</p>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(quiet, icy)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">It is past lights-out, Herr Maat.</div>

  <div class="character">REHM</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Did I ask what time it was?! The Serenade! Put some feeling into it for once!</div>

  <p class="action">Reinhard stands. Slowly places the violin beneath his chin. He plays: a weeping, sentimental Italian salon melody, notes pure and crystalline, his eyes fixed on the blank plaster wall.</p>
  <p class="action">Rehm sways, tears welling in his drunken eyes.</p>

  <div class="character">REHM (CONT'D)</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(sniffing)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">You have touched my soul... you may go back to bed.</div>

  <div class="character">LINA (V.O.)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The entire winter they tormented him like that. From that season on, Reinhard could never bear to hear the Toselli Serenade again. Everything he loved, everything he hated, he confided to that instrument. In his darkest moments, the violin was his only comrade.</div>

</div>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ======================================================= -->
<!-- PAGE 3: MUNICH 1931 — THE CHICKEN FARM                  -->
<!-- ======================================================= -->
<div class="page">

  <div class="slugline">EXT. WALDTRUDERING NEAR MUNICH - DUSK (JUNE 14, 1931)</div>
  <p class="action">A muddy lane on the city outskirts. A ramshackle house with a steeply pitched gabled roof. Chickens peck at wire coops.</p>
  <p class="action">BARON VON EBERSTEIN escorts Reinhard up the wooden steps. Heydrich is in a threadbare civilian suit, recently dishonorably discharged from the navy over a broken engagement.</p>
  <p class="action">A pale, anxious woman—MARGA HIMMLER—opens the screen door.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. HIMMLER BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS</div>
  <p class="action">Smell of camphor and tea. HEINRICH HIMMLER (30) sits propped up in bed in nightwear, round wire spectacles glinting, nursing a feverish flu.</p>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">The Baron speaks highly of your naval service, Heydrich. But we don't need sailors. We need counterintelligence. The party is riddled with police spies and communist agitators.</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">In the Reichsmarine, I specialized in radio transmissions and secure communications, Reichsführer.</div>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="parenthetical">(sliding a fountain pen and a blank notepad across the blanket)</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Take these into the adjoining room. You have thirty minutes. Sketch me an organizational scheme for an independent intelligence service for the SS.</div>

  <div class="slugline">INT. PARLOR - 25 MINUTES LATER</div>
  <p class="action">Reinhard sits alone at a pine table. His hand moves across the pad with mechanical precision: crisp hierarchical branches, card-index systems, courier conduits, counter-surveillance cells.</p>

  <div class="slugline">INT. BEDROOM - MOMENTS LATER</div>
  <p class="action">Himmler examines the sheet. His eyebrows arch behind the round lenses. He looks up at Heydrich.</p>

  <div class="character">HIMMLER</div>
  <div class="dialogue">One hundred and eighty marks a month. You report directly to me. Can you move to Munich immediately?</div>

  <div class="character">HEYDRICH</div>
  <div class="dialogue">Yes, Reichsführer.</div>

  <div class="archival-interlude">
    <div class="cite">[ARCHIVAL FOOTNOTE &bull; DR. WERNER MASER, COMMENTARY NO. 13]</div>
    Lina portrays this as a young unemployed officer merely accepting technical clerical work. In truth, this thirty-minute sketch founded the <em>Sicherheitsdienst</em> (SD)—an extralegal intelligence apparatus that rapidly dismantled judicial protections, orchestrated the purges of 1934, and created the bureaucratic infrastructure for systematic state terror.
  </div>

</div>

